label: Assemble Evidence
description: Join both investigation branches for one entity — the fan-in
input:
  allOf:
    - IdentityContext
    - AssetContext
output: EntityEvidence
examples:
  - given:
      IdentityContext:
        entityId: "e-principal"
        summary: "identity:svc-backup@corp"
      AssetContext:
        entityId: "e-principal"
        summary: "asset:svc-backup@corp"
    expect:
      EntityEvidence:
        entityId: "e-principal"
        identitySummary: "identity:svc-backup@corp"
        assetSummary: "asset:svc-backup@corp"
properties:
  - name: evidence names the entity both branches were about
    description: >-
      Against BOTH inputs, deliberately. Comparing the output to only one of
      them was a false green: an implementation handed IdentityContext for
      Alice and AssetContext for Bob could project Alice and pass, which is
      exactly the cross-entity pairing the property claims to prevent.
      Conjoined, a mismatched bag admits no valid output at all, so the only
      way to satisfy this contract is to reject the input as Failed<In> --
      which turns the property from a claim an implementation can dodge into
      a constraint forcing it to check its inputs cohere.
    expr:
      and:
        - eq:
            - get: output.entityId
            - get: input.IdentityContext.entityId
        - eq:
            - get: output.entityId
            - get: input.AssetContext.entityId
